  "id": "vocab",
  "title": "Vocabulary by theme",
  "priority": 3,
  "order": 9,
  "explanation": "Words that came up in conversation, grouped by theme: home and everyday, people and relationships, feelings, body and health, work and tech, music and studio, weather, describing, and numbers.\n\nA few reminders as you go:\n- **un endroit** is a place; **une place** is a seat or a space\n- **les mouches** (flies) is said \"moosh\", one syllable\n- adjectives ending in -ant describe the thing that causes the feeling (stressant, déprimant, inquiétant); the -é form is how you feel (stressé, déprimé)\n- **malicieux** means mischievous, not malicious; ill-intentioned is malveillant\n- **supportif** is an anglicism Charlotte let through; standard French is *elle ne me soutient pas beaucoup*\n- English business words are used as-is in French offices: business developer, team building, marketing, web designer, le template\n\n**Numbers** are the ones to over-learn: soixante-dix (70) is sixty-ten, quatre-vingts (80) is four-twenties, quatre-vingt-dix (90) is four-twenties-ten, and 21, 31, 41, 51, 61 and 71 take **et un** (vingt et un, soixante et onze) while 81 and 91 don't (quatre-vingt-un, quatre-vingt-onze). Listening drills for numbers arrive with the audio milestone.",
